NAME
       inet_addr, inet_ntoa, inet_pton, inet_ntop, htons - addresses

SYNOPSIS
       #include <arpa/inet.h>

       in_addr_t inet_addr(const char *s);
       char     *inet_ntoa(struct in_addr a);
       int       inet_pton(int af, const char *s, void *dst);
       const char *inet_ntop(int af, const void *src, char *dst, socklen_t n);
       uint32_t  inet_resolve(const char *host);

       uint16_t htons(uint16_t v);   uint16_t ntohs(uint16_t v);
       uint32_t htonl(uint32_t v);   uint32_t ntohl(uint32_t v);

DESCRIPTION
       Converts between text and binary addresses, for IPv4 and, with
       inet_pton and inet_ntop, for IPv6 as well.

       inet_resolve takes either a dotted address or a host name and
       returns the address, going to DNS when it has to, which is what
       ping, wget and the rest use to accept both forms.

       The byte-order functions convert between host and network order
       and are needed for every port number and address written into a
       sockaddr.

RETURN VALUE
       inet_addr returns INADDR_NONE on a malformed address, so it
       cannot report 255.255.255.255; prefer inet_pton in new code.

SEE ALSO
       socket(2), nslookup(1), ping(1)
